Algorithm:

Step 1: Check if the rotation value is zero, if so return the head of the list.
Step 2: Traverse the linked list to find the length of the list and identify the last node.
Step 3: If the list contains only one node, return the head.
Step 4: Connect the last node of the list to the head to form a circular linked list.
Step 5: Reduce the rotation count by taking it modulo with the length of the list.
Step 6: Determine the new position where the list should be broken to form the rotated list.
Step 7: Move both the head pointer and the tail pointer forward until they reach the new positions.
Step 8: Break the circular link by setting the next of the tail to null.
Step 9: Return the updated head pointer as the new head of the rotated list.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)


